docs/architecture/MANIFESTO.md:11:That is the deeper crime. Not complexity alone. Not bloat alone. Enclosure. The slow conversion of programming from direct authorship into supervised participation inside systems built by others. Forced languages. Canonical frameworks. Dependency cathedrals. Libraries hoisted upon you before you have even touched the actual problem. The whole industry leaning over your shoulder, teaching you to think in approved forms.
docs/architecture/MANIFESTO.md:15:The machine used to be yours. Then it became rented. Then remote. Then abstract. Then governed by APIs, platforms, billing dashboards, registries, and invisible revocation powers. Your software no longer ran on a machine you possessed so much as in a territory you temporarily occupied. Computing became tenancy. You could build there, yes—but only through gates, under terms, inside someone else's perimeter.
docs/architecture/MANIFESTO.md:23:The bottleneck in solo projects was never ideas. It was that holding the vision and typing the implementation compete for the same hours and the same mental registers. By the time you've wired the fortieth system, you've half-forgotten the intent behind the twelfth. The detail erodes the design, or the design starves the detail.
docs/architecture/MANIFESTO.md:54:That does not mean the surrounding ecosystem is innocent. It is not. It is extractive. It absorbs whatever it can. It repackages, ingests, centralizes, rehosts, rebrands, and monetizes. So the point is not to pretend that all downstream uses are equally honorable. The point is to keep the code visible without surrendering the meaning of the act.
